{
  "title": "AI enrichment parse failures are deterministic, uncached, and untraceable",
  "description": "Pipeline runs on 2026-06-11 (after purging the mock-poisoned AI cache) \
showed 22 URLs failing with \"AI call failed: Failed to parse response: \
Failed t...\" (see data/extraction-report.md). At the previous commit only 1 \
URL (sweetandsavorymeals.com/buckeye-recipe) failed this way. The failures \
are deterministic: two back-to-back runs failed the exact same URLs at the \
exact same steps (some at enrich_normalize_title, some at \
enrich_generate_description; EnrichAutoTag also drops ~32 URLs per the step \
timing counts). One affected snapshot \
(smittenkitchen-com_2022-04-simplest-mushroom-pasta.json) lost its \
normalized title and description as a result.\n\
\n\
Three sub-problems, in increasing order of weirdness:\n\
\n\
1. Observability: the full error string is never logged anywhere. \
simplify_error() truncates to 50 chars in the report, failed steps write no \
output.json in the run dir, and nothing reaches stdout. Root-causing is \
basically impossible without re-instrumenting. At minimum, log the full \
enrichment error (tracing::warn) and persist failed step output like \
successful ones.\n\
\n\
2. The failing responses are NOT in the cache, but the code says they should \
be: CachingAiClient::complete() caches the response BEFORE the wrapper \
(normalize_title/generate_description/auto_tag) parses the content, and the \
parse error text (\"Failed to parse response: Failed t...\" = \
AiError::ParseError) proves the wrapper was reached. Yet exhaustive scans of \
the cache (invalid JSON, empty content, missing key, null value, wrong type) \
found zero unparseable entries. Something in that story is wrong.\n\
\n\
3. The same prompt inputs succeed when replayed via curl against the real \
OpenRouter endpoint (response_format json_object, temperature 0), albeit \
with an approximated prompt.\n\
\n\
Repro: purge/ignore the AI cache entries for the failing URLs (list in \
data/extraction-report.md under 'AI call failed') and run make pipeline; the \
same URLs fail every time. Worth fixing because each affected URL silently \
ships a degraded snapshot (raw title, no description).",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 2,
  "type": "bug",
